WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.610
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:06.443
I've been in my office for weeks and you people went home?

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.850
Can I just say that putting Maxine Waters in charge

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:15.480
of a financial investigation,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.042
that's just (chef's kiss).

00:00:18.042 --> 00:00:20.220
We have witnesses from many of the key players,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.540
Melvin Capital, Citadel, Reddit, Robinhood,

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:26.010
as well as one of the retail investors involved.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.140
You say his name.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:29.160
Deep you-know-what value.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.430
And we've got Andrew Heaton.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.960
I cashed to the AMC stock my grandpa gave me

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.136
for my birthday, and I made $15!

00:00:35.136 --> 00:00:37.170
Alright, it's our job to ask stupid questions

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.850
usually with malicious intent,

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.240
and it's your job to just run out the clock

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.510
and not really answer them.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:43.850
Does that sound good to you guys?

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:46.790
Congressman, yeah, I appreciate the opportunity

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.770
to address that really important point.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:51.273
Good work.
This is serious oversight responsibility.

00:00:51.273 --> 00:00:53.210
This is just political theater.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:57.000
I have always wanted to play Macbeth in "Julius Caesar."

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.440
This is a serious hearing.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.050
The more times you have to say it's a serious hearing,

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.410
the less serious it is.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:04.900
This is not political theater at all.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.150
It's Kabuki theater. They're very different.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:07.983
I'm not a cat.

00:01:07.983 --> 00:01:09.370
No, you are a scapegoat.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:11.040
Or should I say roaring kitty?

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.170
That was my stripper name.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:14.140
Were you in a naked position?

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.140
Depends if they were paying

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.540
in quarters or bills.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.910
Right now, in my community is so poor

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:21.250
that I have families melting snow

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.580
so they can flush their toilets.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:24.470
Hopefully they get better representation soon.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.530
Compliance is at the center of everything that we do.

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:30.580
That, and paying fines for ignoring regulatory compliance.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:33.840
Some on the left are already floating new restrictions,

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.734
or think we should protect these so-called

00:01:36.734 --> 00:01:40.220
uniformed retail investors—Maxine, Maxine, you need to mute your mic.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.510
Can you mute your, can you mute your mic?

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.740
Okay, we were having a technical problem here.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.625
Somewhere between the keyboard and the chair.

00:01:48.625 --> 00:01:50.010
But I think it has been corrected.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:53.020
I have to be honest. I don't think Congress is smart enough to handle this.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:56.564
Melvin Capital had not engaged in—Maxine, can you—

00:01:56.564 --> 00:01:59.580
Oh, sorry.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.809
Madam Chairwoman, I'm sorry—Maxine?

00:02:01.809 --> 00:02:03.470
I think, I think you're not muted.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:07.730
Can someone under 50 please get to Maxine Waters' office?

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:10.490
We have a few technical difficulties,

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.090
and we'll be right back.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.780
And we are back.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.540
It goes from five dollars, to a hundred dollars, to two hundred dollars.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.370
You decided to halt the buying of GameStock.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.320
It's GameStop.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.080
Your customers wanted to buy the stock.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.130
You wouldn't let them do it

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.210
because you didn't have the capital

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:26.471
to allow them to do it, right?

00:02:26.471 --> 00:02:28.950
Robinhood securities put the restrictions in place

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.650
in an effort to meet increased

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.620
regulatory deposit requirements.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.260
And you materially benefited from it.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.680
It becomes a reverse Robinhood situation.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:37.540
It's in our name.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.220
Rob?
I'm sorry, and I apologize.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.010
How much is your apology worth?

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.930
The public thinks that there was collusion,

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.020
that the big guys, all of you guys,

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.300
were figuring out how to do this

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.790
and ultimately, come out ahead as you always do.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.580
We don't answer to hedge funds.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.440
I answer to my wife and my wife's boyfriend,

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.440
and nobody else.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.330
We had no role—

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:02.179
Melvin Capital played absolutely no role in those trading platform decisions.

00:03:02.179 --> 00:03:05.150
—in Robinhood's decision to limit trading in GameStop.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.160
It all sounds like it's on the up and up.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.750
Melvin Capital was not bailed out

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:10.310
in the midst of these events.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.260
Citadel proactively reached out to become a new investor.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.600
Your fund completely tanks—

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.800
Melvin lost 6 billion dollars in twenty trading days.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.300
—and Citadel comes in to invest in you?

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.650
Yeah, that's not a bailout.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:25.000
Just somebody else gave you capital

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.550
to cover your massive losses

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:27.880
that would've sunk you as a company.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:29.680
And it doesn't smell fishy at all.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.480
Robinhood customers have, have earned

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.050
more than 35 billion dollars. 35 billion dollars is a meaningless number.

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.440
This explains a lot about Congressional budgeting.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.160
Not surprisingly, the situation with GameStock trading—

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:46.140
GameStop—has resulted in knee jerk reactions calling for new laws and regulations.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.970
I didn't hear anyone here today say

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.910
that they were ready to pile on regulations.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:53.120
It's because you're not listening.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.830
It's now House Resolution 328.

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:56.260
Please tell me that's your finger.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.883
Wall Street Tax Act—or your knee—of 2021.

00:03:59.883 --> 00:04:01.980
I actually have a copy of it from the last session.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.710
I am so confused by your biology right now.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:07.180
So I wanna talk about the high-frequency trading.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.833
Only dogs can hear it.

00:04:08.833 --> 00:04:10.050
The majority of Americans support

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.420
taxing Wall Street transactions.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.430
So you're saying a majority of Americans

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.698
think other people should pay more taxes?

00:04:16.698 --> 00:04:19.949
The extreme rise in value of GameStock—

00:04:19.949 --> 00:04:20.950
GameStop—

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:24.710
originated through a Reddit discussion.

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:25.990
I wouldn't call anything that happens

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:27.570
on Wall Street Bets a discussion.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:30.930
It's more like a group of profane howler monkeys

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:32.220
fighting over a grape.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.980
Wall Street Bets may look sophomoric

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:35.190
or chaotic from the outside.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:36.810
You should check out Congress.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:38.190
They are an eccentric community.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:41.175
Basically the Mos Eisley Cantina of financial advice.

00:04:41.175 --> 00:04:43.670
But they're well within the bounds of our content policy.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:47.504
See, you don't say much, you're people lost on HomeStop.

00:04:47.504 --> 00:04:48.490
HomeStop?

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:54.000
Singled out and moved on social media.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.040
Yeah, you can't manipulate stock on social media.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.700
You gotta do that on television!

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.630
The biggest problem facing our people

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:02.766
is of course speech.

00:05:02.766 --> 00:05:05.460
Folks should be able to freely express their views

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.810
on a stock, and they should be able to buy

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:11.720
or not buy a stock based on those views that they may have.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:14.580
Why is the only sensible person here in a gaming chair?

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.950
The retail investor is known as Dumb Money.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:19.660
Hey, retail investor here again.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:20.770
We have to invest in stock

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:22.500
because the Fed has made interest rates so low

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:23.440
we can't save any money.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:25.120
And if we did, it would be eaten up by inflation.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.530
So, either fix that or shut the (beep) up.

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:29.080
Retail investors are in fact

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:30.730
revolutionizing stock markets.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:33.740
I've been burnt once and twice in the market.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:35.800
The problem is you're investing in Boomer stocks.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.240
You need to invest in stocks with a future,

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:41.215
like GameStop, or AMC, or Build-A-Bear.

00:05:41.215 --> 00:05:43.230
You admitted to making mistakes.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:46.858
Specifically what mistakes did you make?

00:05:46.858 --> 00:05:50.040
I admit to always improving.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:51.020
Good save, bud.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.130
And certainly we're not going to be public.

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:56.900
Freudian slip! We're not gonna be perfect.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:00.240
Just tell me straight, is the game rigged?

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:01.530
I believe you're asking

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:06.113
one of the single most important questions posed today.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:20.923
Okay.

00:06:20.923 --> 00:06:21.870
Alright.

